What is a Cordoned Node in Kubernetes?

Cordoning a node means marking a node as unschedulable, so Kubernetes does not place any new pods on that node.

✅ Existing pods continue running.
 ✅ New pods are scheduled on other available nodes.
 ❌ No new workloads are placed on the cordoned node.

Command
Shell
kubectl cordon <node-name>

Example:

Shell
kubectl cordon worker-node-1

Output:

Shell
node/worker-node-1 cordoned
Check Status
Shell
kubectl get nodes
``

Example:

Plain Text
NAME STATUS ROLES
worker-node-1 Ready,SchedulingDisabled <none>
worker-node-2 Ready <none>

SchedulingDisabled indicates the node is cordoned.

When Do We Use Cordoning?
1. Planned Maintenance

Before patching or rebooting a node:

Shell
kubectl cordon worker-node-1

This prevents new pods from landing on the node while you prepare for maintenance.

2. Before Draining

Typical maintenance workflow:

Shell
kubectl cordon worker-node-1
kubectl drain worker-node-1 --ignore-daemonsets
Cordon = stop new pods.
Drain = safely evict existing pods.
3. Troubleshooting

If a node is behaving abnormally:

Shell
kubectl cordon worker-node-1

You can investigate without affecting new application deployments.

Difference Between Cordon and Drain
Command	New Pods Scheduled?	Existing Podscordon	❌ No	✅ Continue running
drain	❌ No	❌ Evicted and moved elsewhere

Think of it as:

Cordon = Close the hotel for new guests. Existing guests stay.
Drain = Ask existing guests to leave and close the hotel.
Uncordon

After maintenance:

Shell
kubectl uncordon worker-node-1

Output:

Shell
node/worker-node-1 uncordoned
 

The scheduler can now place new pods on the node.

Interview Question

Q: What happens if you cordon a node running a Deployment with 10 pods?

Answer: The 10 existing pods continue running on that node. If one of those pods crashes and needs recreation, Kubernetes schedules the replacement pod on another schedulable node because the cordoned node no longer accepts new pods.

For mid-to-senior Kubernetes interviews, remember the maintenance sequence:

Shell
kubectl cordon <node>
kubectl drain <node> --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
# perform maintenance
kubectl uncordon <node>

This is the standard node maintenance workflow used in production clusters.
